// @check
// @error-at 22:2 return type `Id` is declared inside `fresh` and no caller can name it; annotate the return or move `Id` to module scope
// @error-at 26:2 return type `Tok` is declared inside `mkTail` and no caller can name it; annotate the return or move `Tok` to module scope
// @error-at 30:2 return type `Handle` is declared inside `mkFn` and no caller can name it; annotate the return or move `Handle` to module scope
// D1045's NOMINAL-ESCAPE rule — the owner's sub-ruling (DECISIONS.md §"A `type` declared in
// a function body is legal and lexically scoped"), shown the example and answering "seems
// like it should obviously be an error".
//
// A local type is fine; a local NOMINAL type escaping through an INFERRED return is not.
// The asymmetry is the whole point of `new`: nominality is a NAME, and a name that goes out
// of scope takes the nominal identity with it, so the inferred return would be a type no
// caller can write down. A local STRUCTURAL type escapes freely as its shape — the shape IS
// the type and the caller can spell it — which the sibling `body-scope-local-struct-escapes`
// runs.
//
// THREE SPELLINGS OF ONE RULE, because "the inferred return" is three syntactic things: an
// explicit `return`, the implicit trailing-expression return, and a nominal buried INSIDE
// the inferred type rather than being it (the returned CLOSURE, `() => Handle`). The third
// is the reason the escape predicate descends a `TyFunc`: `() => Handle` names something out
// of scope just as surely as a bare `Handle` does.
function fresh() {
  type Id = new i32
  return 7 as Id
}
function mkTail() {
  type Tok = new i32
  9 as Tok
}
function mkFn() {
  type Handle = new i32
  return (): Handle => 1 as Handle
}
print(fresh() as i32)
print(mkTail() as i32)
print(mkFn()() as i32)
